Cookies written a second at a time (Diego, 15 November 2026: "fix the
cache and see why are who storing all that data"). One load of The Verge
set 261 cookies, each a transaction of its own: SQLite's journal made,
written, fsynced and removed, and the database fsynced - 522 disk commits,
129 MB written to the device for 13 MB of cookies, 15.7 s of a 32 s load
(Kosmos testing.md 18.836). WebKit asks for WAL, which makes this cheap
elsewhere; WAL needs memory shared between processes, which SQLite's
dotfile VFS on Kosmos has not got, so the database stayed in DELETE mode.
The changes now go into one transaction, committed a second after its
first change and when the database closes, as Chromium keeps cookies. A
crash loses the last second's cookies and never the database.

